{"qid":"Q422065","names":["Sultanate of Sulu"],"title":"Sultanate of Sulu","endonym":{"native":"سلطنة سولو","lang":"ar"},"capsule":"Founded around 1457 by Sharif ul-Hashim after his arrival from Sumatra, the Sultanate of Sulu governed the Sulu Archipelago, parts of Mindanao, and much of northern Borneo from its capital at Maimbung and later Jolo. At its apex in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries it was a major entrepôt in the Southeast Asian maritime trade network and a persistent military rival to Spanish Manila, raiding as far as the Visayas and Luzon. Spain finally captured Jolo in 1876 and formally annexed the sultanate's remaining territories by 1878; the 1880 boundary reflects the last period of recognized autonomous sovereignty before full colonial absorption. The institution of the sultanate persisted nominally but without political power into the twentieth century.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core island territories around Jolo are well documented in Spanish colonial records and in later American ethnographic surveys; extent of claimed mainland Borneo suzerainty is less precisely bounded.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Sultanate of Sulu","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sultanate_of_Sulu"},{"label":"Britannica: Sultanate of Sulu","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Sultanate-of-Sulu"},{"label":"James Francis Warren, The Sulu Zone 1768–1898 (NUS Press, 2007)","url":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctv1ntj0q"},{"label":"Najeeb M. Saleeby, The History of Sulu (Bureau of Printing, Manila, 1908) — archive.org","url":"https://archive.org/details/historyofsulu00sale"}